From: Cromite <cromite@cromite.org>
Date: Sat, 31 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: Add headless automation mode

Adds an Android "headless / show-on-demand" automation mode driven by the
existing cromite test-support DevTools socket (WebDriverIO + Appium).

When the "headless-automation-mode" cromite flag is enabled, an opaque
overlay can be placed on top of the web contents so the rendered page is
hidden from the user while the renderer keeps running (so JavaScript and
automation continue to work). The overlay is toggled on demand from the
existing window.cromite private API:

    await window.cromite.setContentVisible(false); // hide
    await window.cromite.setContentVisible(true);  // show
    await window.cromite.isContentVisible();        // query

The visibility methods are exposed on chrome://version, exactly like the
other window.cromite test APIs, and reach the Android view layer through a
small JNI bridge that operates on the WebContents container view (no
ChromeActivity coupling). Hiding uses an opaque Android View placed above
the compositor surface rather than detaching the surface, so compositing
is not suspended.

This patch builds on Add-cromite-test-support.patch (it extends the
CromitePrivateApiExtension mojo interface, the browser-process host and
the renderer binding it introduces) and must be applied after it.

Build note: the new Java class CromiteHeadlessBridge must be registered
with the chrome generate_jni target in the build environment so that
chrome/browser can include CromiteHeadlessBridge_jni.h. The exact
generate_jni "sources" list in the targeted Chromium version is added
there, not in this patch.

